        <!-- Advantages -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">25. Advantages of Records</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>Very concise syntax.</li>
            <li>Less boilerplate code.</li>
            <li>Built-in accessors.</li>
            <li>Built-in equals and hashCode.</li>
            <li>Useful toString implementation.</li>
            <li>Good fit for immutable data carriers.</li>
            <li>Excellent for DTOs and API models.</li>
            <li>Can implement interfaces.</li>
            <li>Can contain validation and business-related helper methods.</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- Limitations -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">26. Limitations of Records</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>Cannot extend another class.</li>
            <li>Are implicitly final.</li>
            <li>Not suitable for highly mutable domain objects.</li>
            <li>Provide shallow rather than deep immutability.</li>
            <li>Should not be used merely because they are shorter.</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- Best practices -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">27. Best Practices</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>Use records for data-centric models.</li>
            <li>Use compact constructors for validation.</li>
            <li>Keep record methods focused and simple.</li>
            <li>Use normal classes when the object requires mutable state or inheritance.</li>
            <li>Use records for DTOs when the application architecture benefits from immutable data.</li>
            <li>Remember that referenced mutable objects are not automatically deeply immutable.</li>
        </ul>

